<section id="about" class="about shell">
  <div class="edge-pad about-grid">
    <div class="about-index">
      <span class="section-label">01 / About</span>
      <span class="mono big-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
    </div>
    <div class="about-body">
      <p class="lede">
        Two threads run through what I build: agentic systems that make AI
        teams faster, and models that make sense of medical data too dense
        for a human to read by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        At TCS, that meant designing SuPRvisor, a serverless multi-agent
        platform that replaced a 100-person team's 24–48 hour manual review
        process — the unglamorous, load-bearing kind of AI work that
        doesn't make headlines but has to actually ship, on someone else's
        production timeline. At Emory, the same instinct — break a problem
        too large for one model into pieces a pipeline can actually handle
        — shows up in my research: turning gigapixel pathology slides
        (.svs) into interpretable tumor maps, and more recently, working
        with fMRI data.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm drawn to problems that sit at the boundary of the model and the
        system around it, where a benchmark score matters less than whether
        the thing holds up against real, messy, high-stakes data in
        production.
      </p>
      <div class="focus-tags">
        <span class="tag">Agentic AI</span>
        <span class="tag">Medical Imaging (WSI)</span>
        <span class="tag">fMRI / Neuroimaging</span>
        <span class="tag">Serverless / AWS</span>
        <span class="tag">Applied ML</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="rule"></div>
</section>

<style>
